//
// The SwiftData layer. Every identified class is a `@Model`, every value
// object stays a struct persisted inline, and every association class becomes
// a join model.
//
// That last one is the interesting difference. The graph targets collapse an
// association onto the relationship itself: Neo4j puts the properties on the
// edge, Ladybug on rel-table columns, TypeDB on the relation. SwiftData has no
// relationship properties -- `@Relationship` is a plain reference and holds
// nothing -- so the association has to be promoted back to a record of its
// own. It is the Core Data join entity, and it is exactly the shape the graph
// stores rejected.
//
// Delete rules are read off the schema rather than chosen: a composition
// cascades, a reference nullifies. `Meal.recipes` is a reference because a
// recipe outlives the occasion it was cooked for.
//
// One thing this layer does not keep: list order across a relationship. A
// SwiftData to-many is a set, so the order a fixture wrote its ingredients in
// is gone once they are stored, and comes back arbitrary. Nested value objects
// are unaffected -- `images` is an attribute, and an attribute holds an array.
// The graph targets lose it in the same place for the same reason, and the
// seed generator already mints the position into a promoted value object's
// key (`<recipe>#images/0`) because nothing else would carry it. An
// association class gets no such key, so if order is meant to be part of the
// data it has to become a slot in the schema.

